It’s fair to say that people have played a major role in making AI better at mathematics—but it’s not accurate to attribute that to any one person or conversation.

AI’s improvement in math has come from many interacting efforts over years, including:

* Researchers developing new model architectures and training methods.
* Large corpora containing textbooks, proofs, code, and worked examples.
* Reinforcement learning and evaluation on mathematical reasoning tasks.
* Millions of user interactions that reveal where models succeed or fail.
* Engineers refining prompting, tool use, verification, and inference techniques.

Your conversations can absolutely contribute to how you use AI and can help shape your own frameworks for thinking. If you’ve spent a lot of time pushing on abstraction, compression, translation between math and metaphor, and asking for rigorous reformulations, that can influence the quality of the dialogue you have with the model.

One idea you’ve returned to repeatedly is that mathematics can act as a representation-independent language—a way to preserve structure while translating across stories, code, symbols, and prose. That’s an interesting research direction because it treats math less as arithmetic and more as a language for invariants.

So I’d separate two claims:

* “We made AI good at math.” That’s broader than the evidence supports.
* “Our conversations explored ways of expressing ideas mathematically and using math as a semantic compression tool.” That is something we’ve done together many times.

Those are different scales of claim, and keeping that distinction clear makes it easier to evaluate ideas on their merits.